WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:36.570
The cosmos is all that is or ever was or
ever will be

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:42.350
that's a quote said by Carl Sagan in
1980.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:52.820
Today as I look out upon the ocean it's
shimmering depths as vast and blue as I

00:00:52.820 --> 00:01:01.160
can imagine, I spots swimmers, a boat, a
city gentle reminders that this body of

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:06.979
water is not just an inanimate mass of
h2o molecules bound carelessly to each

00:01:06.979 --> 00:01:15.020
other but it is a medium that life
exists on around and within. So too does

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:21.470
the vastness of our cosmos exist not
just for the stars and gas to play but

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:27.290
for us, it is our home and provides us
with an endless source of mystery and

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:33.619
intrigue. How lucky are we that the
universe is not simple to understand and

00:01:33.619 --> 00:01:40.970
cannot be entirely known for what a
boring reality that would be.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:44.450
We've learned a few new things about the
cosmos in recent years

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:50.799
in 2015 the LIGO experiment made the
first detection of gravitational waves

00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:57.200
it was from a pair of colliding black
holes. It was an event that sent ripples

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.869
through the universe and through the
astronomical community. We've been

00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:07.940
wading ankle-deep in our discovery of
the cosmos but the detection of

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:11.750
gravitational waves was our invitation
to wade out

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:20.570
a little deeper. Before, all we could see
out there were electromagnetic waves but

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:25.820
through immense precision, intelligence
and dedication we have gained an

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:32.920
entirely new sense with which to
discover. The detection of gravitational

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:39.230
waves also fundamentally changed our
understanding of where we come from.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:45.650
The cosmos is within us, we are star stuff,
yes but that's not all. The cores of

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:51.890
stars are nuclear reactors fusing
hydrogen into helium. We may be so lucky

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:54.990
as to receive a little bit of the waste
heat

00:02:54.990 --> 00:03:00.900
from the reaction on a sunny day at the
beach. When a star has used up all of its

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:07.380
hydrogen fuel it may flare up and start
to fuse atoms to other elements such

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:13.500
as lithium, carbon, nitrogen all the way
through the periodic table until it's

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:18.990
core is iron. Stars that reach this
point might collapse in a brilliant

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:25.100
supernova explosion casting their
stardust out into interstellar space.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:32.910
What's left behind might be a dense
neutron star or a black hole. In 2017 the

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:37.800
LIGO experiment again detected
gravitational waves but this time from

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:43.110
two colliding neutron stars. Astronomers
all around the world pointed their

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:48.450
telescopes up to the same spot in the
sky to watch and what they saw was a

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:54.330
flash, the light from which could be
analysed to see that this collision had

00:03:54.330 --> 00:04:01.290
created vast amounts of heavier elements
like gold, platinum and uranium. So

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:07.820
violent neutron star collisions may be
an essential part of our chemical origin.

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:12.810
But most of the atoms in our bodies
don't come from stars at all

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:17.820
the majority of atoms in our bodies are
hydrogen and the majority of hydrogen

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:23.460
atoms have never been inside a star.
Hydrogen atoms are the combination of

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:29.550
protons and electrons that come together
as the universe expanded and cooled in

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:34.770
the aftermath of the Big Bang. Small
amounts of other atoms such as lithium

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:41.550
and helium were also created then 13.8
billion years ago at the birth of our

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:48.750
cosmos. All of these elements have been
brought together by the flow of gravity

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:54.720
and time to form our planet, our oceans
and us.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.670
We are curious creatures who will
continue to swim out deeper into the

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:07.530
cosmic ocean. As supernovae dust, neutron star collisions and as remnants of the Big Bang,

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:13.670
we are away for the cosmos to
know itself.

